# CLI: commands, the stdout contract, exit codes

## Thin commands

- A command in `src/commands/<name>.ts` is `Command.make(name, { ...flags, ...args }, handler)` with a description, added to the subcommands in `src/cli.ts`. The handler decodes its input (a schema from `src/schemas/` for an argument, flag value, or stdin), runs one program from `src/core/`, and renders the result through `Output`. No business logic, no HTTP, no config parsing in the handler.
- Flags shared across commands (`--json`) are defined once and reused, so they mean the same thing everywhere.
- Config is loaded inside the program that needs it, never at the top of `main`: `--help`, `--version`, and `doctor` must work with no credentials and a broken config file.
- Reading piped stdin returns nothing at a terminal, so a command never blocks on a keyboard it didn't ask for. A command that needs a terminal fails with a usage error (exit 2) when stdin or stdout isn't one.
- Code that only some commands need and that can't load on Node (an interactive UI, a native library) is imported dynamically inside the handler, so vitest and every other command never load it.

## Platform boundary

vitest runs every unit test on Node, so only `src/bin.ts`, `src/platform/`, and paths an adapter declares in `.claude/platform-paths.json` may use `bun`, `bun:*`, the `Bun` global, or `@effect/platform-bun`. Everything else uses Effect's `FileSystem`, `Path`, `Stdio`, `Terminal`, and `HttpClient`, provided by `BunServices` in `bin.ts` and `NodeServices` in tests; `Host` (platform, executable path, compiled flag) comes from `bin.ts` too. `process.env` is read only in `src/config.ts` and `bin.ts`. The `guard-boundaries` hook denies these before the write and `biome-plugins/boundaries.grit` fails lint on them. If a rule is wrong for a case, change both, don't route around them.

## stdout contract

stdout carries results only; `{{project_slug}} ... | jq` and any protocol mode a later command adds depend on it.

- Results go through the `Output` service. Notes for the person (a hint, a summary line) go through `Output.note`, which writes stderr. Logs go to stderr and the log file (`logging.ts`). No `console.*` anywhere in `src/`; `process.stdout` only in `bin.ts`, and `process.stderr` only in `bin.ts`, `logging.ts` (the terminal sink), and `config.ts` (the color default's TTY check). Both guards deny the rest.
- Effect's CLI module prints help, `--version`, completions, and (with error rendering on) parse errors through Effect's `Console`, a `Context.Reference` whose default is the global console, so all of it lands on stdout, including the help it prints for a usage error. `main` runs the command with error rendering off and a holding `Console`, and flushes what it held to stdout only for `ok`/`help` outcomes; errors are rendered by `main` on stderr, as `{"error":{tag,message,retryable}}` when argv contains `--json` (parse errors happen before any handler sees its flags).
- The runtime's default error reporting logs the failure through the default logger, which is `console.log`: run the program with error reporting disabled, and provide the loggers at the outermost layer so nothing Effect logs reaches stdout.
- With `--json`: one JSON value for a result, NDJSON for a stream. A new output shape goes in `src/schemas/`.
- No ANSI on stdout when it isn't a TTY; colored stderr notes are off with `NO_COLOR` or a non-TTY stderr. A closed pipe (`{{project_slug}} doctor | head -1`) ends the run quietly (EPIPE is an interrupt, not a defect).

## Exit codes

`exitCodeFor` in `src/errors.ts` is the only mapping: 0 ok, `--help`, and a bare `{{project_slug}}`; 1 defect; 2 usage, bad input, not found; 3 not configured, invalid config; 4 upstream unavailable; 5 invalid model output; 6 permission denied; 130 interrupted by a signal. They are documented in the README; changing one is a breaking change for scripts. A new tagged error needs a case in `exitCodeFor` and `retryableFor` (both exhaustive) and a test that asserts its code through `runCli`.

## Logging per invocation

`main` logs one `command` line per run, including parse errors and `--version`: the subcommand, flag names (never values), `exitCode`, `durationMs`, `runId`, `errorTag`. Don't add another per-command line. `--log-level` (built in) overrides `LOG_LEVEL` for one run.

## Tests

Every command gets argv-level tests through `runCli` (`tests/helpers/cli.ts`), which runs the same `main` and `AppLayer` on Node with stdin, stdout, stderr, and logs captured. Assert the exit code, that stdout holds only the result (and is empty on failure), the `--json` shape, and the stderr message. Keep the cross-command contract (bad flag, `--json` errors, help to stdout, exit codes) in one contract test file.
